What was taken, and where it lives now:

- The content-scoring algorithm, now in
  `packages/core/src/clarity/extract.ts`. Specifically: the
  `unlikelyCandidates` and `okMaybeItsACandidate` regexes and the `positive` /
  `negative` class-and-id regexes, copied verbatim; the multi-script comma
  list; the paragraph score `1 + text.split(commas).length +
  min(floor(length / 100), 3)`, including the `split().length` off-by-one that
  makes a comma-free paragraph score 1 rather than 0; the tag base scores
  (`DIV +5`, `PRE`/`TD`/`BLOCKQUOTE +3`, `ADDRESS`/`OL`/`UL`/`DL`/`DD`/`DT`/
  `LI`/`FORM -3`, `H1`-`H6`/`TH -5`); the class-and-id weight of plus or minus
  25 per regex per attribute, scored independently on className and id; the
  25-character and has-an-ancestor gates; the `DEFAULT_TAGS_TO_SCORE` set,
  which deliberately omits `h1`; the five-ancestor propagation with dividers
  1, 2, 6, 9, 12; the link-density penalty `score * (1 - linkDensity)` with
  same-page anchors counted at 0.3; and the sibling-gathering threshold
  `max(10, 0.2 * topScore)`.

- The shape of `isProbablyReaderable`'s cheap gate, in the same file.

No Readability source file is redistributed, and the library is not a
dependency. What was taken is the algorithm, reimplemented against a different
output: Readability returns cleaned HTML, and `_prepArticle` discards data
tables, figures split from their captions, code blocks, footnotes, math and
pull quotes on the way. This emits an ordered list of typed blocks that keeps
all of those, each carrying a pointer back to the node it came from.

